> ### &#128161; Note: Some tips for working with JDBC
> 
> JDBC objects, such as Connection, Statement, PreparedStatement and
> ResultSet are resources that must be closed after they become unnecessary.
> A "wider-scoped" object closes the other resources that were created with
> it. For example, closing a Connection closes Statements and closing
> a Statement closes the returned ResultSet. It is suggested that you
> keep the Connection open until the end of the program but you should
> wrap Statements and PreparedStatement into a try-with-resources block.
> There is no need to close ResultSets because short after they are
> processed, the (Prepared)Statement will also be closed.
> For more information, read: https://blogs.oracle.com/weblogicserver/using-try-with-resources-with-jdbc-objects
>
> For queries that do not have parameters to substitute, you can just use
> Statement. For queries that do have parameter substitutions, you should use
> PreparedStatements and set parameters with setString(), setInt() etc.
> It is much more safe. Adding parameters with normal String operations
> may result in code vulnerable to SQL injection attacks. Also, this might prove more efficient and faster to run.
>
> Some old tutorials state that you must load the JDBC driver with
> Class.forName() first. Since Java 6 this step is not necessary anymore. However you may find, that it still happens in the background if you go inside the Drivers constructor method.
> 
>You can log how the queries look like after parameter substitutions if you
> call toString() on the PreparedStatement object.
> 
>It is recommended to encapsulate database manipulation logic into a single
> class. In this way, your code will be easy to read and you can easily
> replace the database technology as well.  
> 
> Sometimes, an interface is also introduced, this is called the DAO pattern: http://best-practice-software-engineering.ifs.tuwien.ac.at/patterns/dao.html

