#### Hands-on: JPA configuration and transaction handling

Work in the ***04-jpa-webshop*** module.  

Let's make a webshop application! We have a base code but still missing a lot of things. Let

1. The persistence.xml file is provided in the main/resources/META-INF folder
   for your convenience. Let us review what is in this file!

2. We need to annotate the Product class in order to save its instances with JPA.
   Note the @GeneratedValue annotation that is used to generate IDs automatically.
   If we save an instance with its ID set to null, the JPA provider will generate a unique value.

3. In the Webshop class, we initialize JPA. First, we create an EntityManagerFactory and with the help of that,
   we instantiate an EntityManager.  We will invoke database operations on this object.
   Don't forget to close the EntityManager after the database operation!
   
    ```java
    EntityManager entityManager = entityManagerFactory.createEntityManager();
    
    // your JPA code comes here...
    
    entityManager.close();
    ```
   
    > ### &#128161; EntityManager object is NOT thread-safe! 
    Always create a new one and don't reuse it after closing it. 
    Read about thread safety: https://stackoverflow.com/questions/261683/what-is-meant-by-thread-safe-code


4. JPA *write* (create, update, delete) operations must run in a transaction.
   For this, we also need an EntityTransaction object.  You will learn
   more about transactions later, for now, just remember to wrap your code
   inside a transaction if you use a write operation:

    ```java
    EntityTransaction transaction = entityManager.getTransaction();
    transaction.begin();

    // your JPA code comes here...

    transaction.commit();
    ```

5. Now we have everything to implement our first two operations.
   For saveProduct(), we need to start a transactions since it is going to be
   a write operation.  Then we use em.persist() to save this new instance
   to the database and finally, we commit the transaction.

6. Now let us connect this code to the text user interface and try it out!

7. Ok, we also have to list all the Products. We are using a new thing called JPQL here, 
   which is almost like SQL but for objects (we can use our entities in the place of the tables). 

