#### Workshop: Transaction rollback

Continue working in the ***04-jpa-webshop*** module. 

For consistency problems, transactions are your friends. Imagine if you would have a product inventory
too, start saving the order and in the middle of the process you would realise that one of the items are out of stock.
You would have to remove the order from the database and put all the items already in it back to the inventory. To do
this manually would be a tedious task.  
Or you ask for the id of the customer as an input, but you get an invalid id, and only realize this when you query for 
the customer from the database.
Fortunately we already have a tool in databases that can help with this (and many other things as well): transactions!

Let's see how we can handle rolling back transaction in the case of an SQLException:
- start a try-catch block after beginning the transaction
- if the try branch executes without problem, commit the transaction
- if an exception is thrown, log it to the console (e.printStacktrace()) and 
roll back the transaction (transaction.rollback())
- and if something else happens (like not finding a customer with the id you got as parameter) you can also roll back
the transaction

