### Testing

#### Note: Testing JPA code

You may have asked yourself the question: how can I test JPA code easily?
If you use the same configuration for testing, the database will be
required for testing.  Furthermore, you will always need to restore a clean
state of the database after tests.  For example, if you add a product and
assert that there is only one product in the database, it will work first but
after running the test again, you will have two products in the database.
Because of this, usually a different approach is used for testing.

The easiest way to test your JPA code is to use an in-memory database
for testing.  It will be started along with your test and every time it
is going to be empty.  You do not have to bother with cleanup.  The most
common in-memory database that is used is HSQL.

